# YMYL (pet health) disclaimers and safety

Pet health content can cause real harm if wrong. Google and AdSense reviewers treat it with extra scrutiny (`docs/SEO-PLAYBOOK.md` sections 3 and 6; `docs/ADSENSE.md`).

## Required on every tool and guide page
- `<Disclaimer />` ("Not veterinary advice...") is rendered. `ToolPageShell` and `GuidePageShell` include it; any custom page must add it. `audit:seo` (`tool-disclaimer`) and `audit:adsense` (`disclaimer`) fail without it.
- `<ReviewerBox />` and `<AuthorMeta />` are present. The reviewer box must state **pending review** unless `siteConfig.reviewerPlaceholder` has been replaced by a real, named, credentialed reviewer who actually reviewed that page. Never fabricate a reviewer, license, or credential.
- A sources list with primary veterinary references (`src/data/sources.ts`).
- An "Assumptions and limits" section: who the estimate fits, who it does not (mixed breeds, puppies/kittens, seniors, pregnancy, illness, medical diets), and when to contact a veterinarian.
- Embed pages keep the "Educational only, not veterinary advice" line in `EmbedFrame.astro`.

## Tone and wording
- Use "estimate", "typical range", "starting point", "talk to your veterinarian". Never "diagnose", "treat", "cure", "guaranteed", "100% safe", "no need to see a vet", "instead of your vet".
- Never give medication names, doses or home treatment instructions. Never induce-vomiting advice. For poisoning content the only action is: contact your veterinarian, an emergency clinic, or a poison hotline immediately (ASPCA Animal Poison Control Center, Pet Poison Helpline); verify any phone number/fee on the official page on the publish date.
- Weight-loss, feeding for medical conditions, pregnancy/lactation feeding, homemade/raw diets: state that a veterinarian (or board-certified veterinary nutritionist for home-prepared diets) must individualise the plan.

## Emergency-adjacent tools and guides (chocolate/toxins, pregnancy complications, dehydration)
- The "call your vet / poison hotline now" instruction appears in the HTML before any numeric result, not only after JS runs.
- Results are framed as "possible risk level for planning a call", never "safe amount". Do not show a "safe dose".
- No ads between the warning and the tool, and no affiliate content.
- Fail closed: if inputs are missing or out of range, hide results (the components call `showResults(panel, false)`), do not guess.

## Formulas and data
- Every constant has a citation in the JSDoc and on the page. If sources disagree, show the range and cite both.
- Unit tests include a published worked example and boundary values (`src/lib/formulas/formulas.test.ts`).
- Round conservatively; never present false precision (1-2 significant digits for ranges).
- Changing a formula or constant requires `vet-fact-checker` review and a page-copy update in the same PR.

## Privacy
- Calculator inputs never leave the browser: no `fetch`, no analytics events, no storage beyond the URL query string. If that ever changes, update `/privacy/` first and re-run `adsense-readiness-audit`.

## Do not
- Do not hide or shrink the disclaimer, collapse it by default, or move it below the fold on emergency pages.
- Do not claim endorsements ("AAHA approved"); say "based on".
- Do not add "reviewed by" JSON-LD (`reviewedBy`) unless a real reviewer reviewed the page.
